INSERT INTO feature VALUES('parallel-checking','incremental','Parallel checking over supports','incremental-checking','partial','notable',NULL,'Two checks reorder freely when neither writes what the other reads, so a scheduler over the dependence graph checks and replays in parallel. `gandr check` on more than one thread runs the checks of each readmission on its pool: the calling thread releases each declaration in module order and settles each check as it returns, and a declaration that names a running one waits for it. At each width above one, a declaration that names a running plain definition, released at the frontier, reads the entry that the definition''s commit makes instead, and runs beside it. That commit compares the two entries, and a refusal or another entry takes the prediction back and drops each later release. The calling thread releases a check only while a slot holds it, a lane''s ring or its own one slot, so the checks ahead of the settles stay within the rings'' capacity and one more, and what they hold stays a bound of the width. A check shares the slots of the entry log, so a commit beside it copies a short run of slots, and a compaction keeps the layers of the session to the open checks and a logarithm of the session. A declaration with no rank of its own can take the rank that it has when each earlier release admits, and the fold moves it when one is refused; `gandr check` makes that prediction at each width above one. A readmission that keeps the entries outside the reach of an edit keeps and retires them beside the running checks, each at its own rank. The kernel holds a keep while a running check reads into its key or its name. A retirement waits while a running check reaches the rank of an entry that it retires. A module whose pair the kernel may leave undecided runs on the pool too: a check that leaves its pair undecided drops each later release, and the answer of the pair takes the serial position before each of them crosses again. `gandr check` of fewer sources than its threads checks apart the code obligations of each last drain that holds 64 of them or more, in runs of 16 or more, one run for each thread. Each gives the verdict of the serial check. Past 256 expansions of a check, it also forks the sibling goals of one frame at no root and under no fixpoint body, one part for each goal, and folds the parts in the order of the serial walk. Two parts that name one type by two nodes agree by content. `check:differential` runs the strict root of the corpus at 2, 4 and 16 jobs and at the width of the host. At each width, each verdict, each refusal and each sharing count equals the serial run, and each declaration expands at least as much.','core-checker-bridge kernel-check-memo kernel-core kernel-core-base surface-dispatcher',NULL,'corpus/ledger/features/parallel-checking.gandr','#320');
